# Adding New Rows to a Model Table in Laravel

When you need to persist a new record to your database, such as creating a user from a form submission, the approach depends heavily on how much data validation logic and business rules you require. The most robust pattern in Laravel involves utilizing model binding combined with explicit mass assignment protection, rather than relying solely on raw `create()` calls or deprecated methods like `setAttribute()`.

The core mechanism for adding a new row is the `fill()` method followed by calling `save()` on your model instance. However, simply passing an array to `fill()` will fail if your `$fillable` property hasn't been configured correctly. By default, Laravel disables mass assignment to prevent accidental database updates via untrusted input. This means you must explicitly define which attributes the model is allowed to receive when creating a new instance or updating an existing one.

```php
class User extends Model
{
    protected $fillable = ['name', 'email', 'password'];
}
```

Once the `$fillable` array is set, you can instantiate your model and pass the request data directly into it. This is often cleaner than manually creating a new instance and assigning properties one by one.

```php
$user = new User($request->only(['name', 'email', 'password']));
$user->save();
```

This approach works well for simple scenarios, but it bypasses the `boot()` methods defined in your model. If you have custom logic inside `User::boot()` that initializes relationships or sets default timestamps, that code won't run during creation unless you handle it explicitly. To ensure full lifecycle management, including relationship initialization and event triggers, you should use the `fill()` method:

```php
$user = new User();
$user->name = $request->name;
$user->email = $request->email;
$user->password = Hash::make($request->password);
$user->save();
```

While this looks verbose, it guarantees that all model-level hooks are executed. The `boot()` method is particularly useful here because it runs automatically whenever a new instance is created via the factory or direct instantiation. This allows you to set default values for columns like `created_at` or initialize many-to-many relationships without cluttering your controller logic.

For password handling, never store plain text. Always hash passwords using the `Hash` facade before saving. Laravel's built-in hashing strategies ensure that even if your database is compromised, the raw credentials remain unreadable. You can also leverage the `User::create()` static method, which is essentially a shorthand for creating an instance and saving it in one step.

```php
User::create([
    'name' => $request->name,
    'email' => $request->email,
    'password' => Hash::make($request->password),
]);
```

This static method is efficient for straightforward creation tasks but still respects the `$fillable` constraint. If you attempt to pass an attribute outside of this array, Laravel will throw a `MassAssignmentException`. Ignoring this exception or disabling mass assignment globally (`$this->unguard()`) should be treated as a security anti-pattern unless you have a specific, audited reason to do so.

If your application grows in complexity, consider using Laravel's Form Request classes to separate validation logic from controller concerns. This keeps your codebase organized and ensures that only validated data reaches the model layer. Additionally, while third-party tutorials can be helpful, always verify them against the official documentation to ensure compatibility with your specific Laravel version, as API behaviors often change between minor releases. For deeper dives into these patterns, resources like [LaravelCompany](https://laravelcompany.com) offer curated guides that align closely with modern best practices.
